          "text": "The user is asking about the binary size implications of separate compilation vs header-only (inline) linking. Let me think about this carefully.\n\nCurrent state (header-only with `inline`/`static inline` functions in headers):\n- Each `.obj` file contains its own copy of the inline function\n- After linking, the linker may deduplicate these (especially with `/OPT:ICF` in Release), but in Debug (which is what they use - `/Od /Zi /MDd`) it likely doesn't\n- Actually, `inline` in C++ doesn't force the compiler to inline - it's a hint. The function still has external linkage and a single copy in the final binary if the compiler chooses to not inline it. But `static inline` in C means each translation unit gets its own private copy.\n\nCurrent state: `string_utils.hpp` has `inline` functions (C++), `string_utils.h` has `static inline` functions (C).\n\nFor the C++ `inline` functions: the compiler may or may not inline them. The function has external linkage and one copy is chosen (the \"inline\" keyword in C++ means \"may be defined in multiple translation units, pick one\"). So in C++, the linker will deduplicate.\n\nFor the C `static inline` functions: each translation unit gets its own copy, because `static` means internal linkage. So `main.obj` has one copy of `parse_point`, `ref.obj` has another copy. After linking, they're separate functions, but only used within their own object files.\n\nWait, but currently there's only ONE source file per project (main.c or main.cpp), no ref and main in the same project. So there's no duplication within a single program. The duplication is across programs (t1 main has parse_point, t3 main has parse_point, t4 main has parse_point).\n\nSo the question is: within a single executable, will separate compilation make it smaller?\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The user currently has:\n- For each task: one `main.cpp` → one `main.obj` → one `main.exe`\n- The `inline` functions from string_utils.hpp are inlined into main.obj\n\nSo for a single executable: whether functions are in headers (inlined) or in separate .obj files, the executable will contain one copy of the function code. If the compiler inlines it, the code disappears into call sites. If not, it stays as a separate function body.\n\nThe main difference: with header-only, changing string_utils recompiles everything. The binary size difference for a single program is negligible.\n\nThe real benefit is:\n1. Rebuild speed (incremental compilation)\n2. Organization/separation of concerns\n3. Ability to create libraries (.lib/.dll) shared across projects\n\nLet me explain this to the user clearly.",
