          "text": "The user is asking why `clang-format` moved `#include \"string_utils.h\"` to be first. This is actually the correct behavior per Google/LLVM style.\n\nThe standard ordering convention used by clang-format (and recommended by AGENTS.md §4.9 п.8) is:\n\n> **Порядок `#include` (Google-стиль):** свой header первым (тест self-containment) → пустая строка → C system → C++ stdlib → сторонние → проектные.\n\nSo the `.c` file's own header goes first. This is intentional: it verifies that the header is self-contained (can be compiled without depending on other includes before it). If `string_utils.h` had a missing include, it would fail at compile time right here, rather than silently working in `main.c` where the dependency happens to be included earlier.\n\nThis is exactly what the user noticed: clang-format reordered includes to match this convention. It's a feature, not a bug.",
